# The disks' speed on MAME's AT, against budgets (roadmap disk-scheduler.md
# M1, M2; tests/posix/rdtime.c): MAME's diskettes turn at the drives' speeds
# and its CPU at the AT's, so the budgets are about the drivers' and the OS
# server's work; Bochs and DOSBox-X give no AT's time. M1: 32 KB written to D
# in calls of 4 KB within 1 s (1.86 s before M1's memcpy; 0.74 s in E7).
# M2: A's KERNEL.BIN at 96 KB in 6 s or better, scaled to its size, since
# it grows with each milestone; B's 45 KB written and read back within 8 s
# each; C's NUMBERS.TXT within 0.6 s in calls of 15 KB and 3 s in calls of
# 512 bytes; and a sector of a run within 3 ms. M3's disk server holds
# them all. M6 tightens them to what it reaches, with a tenth or more to
# spare: B read back (from the disk server's cache) within 1 s, C's
# NUMBERS.TXT within 0.5 s in calls of 15 KB and 1.1 s in calls of 512
# bytes, a sector of a run within 2.6 ms (M6: 260, 420, 980 ms, 2.37 ms).
# D's write in calls of 4 KB, 920 ms, keeps its 1 s; B's, 7.2-8.0 s with
# the diskette's rotation, its 8 s. M8: /mnt's names are learnt before the
# first check, and B's write and read back include the look at its
# diskette that M8 adds (B: 7.8 s, 0.48 s).
program: idle
define: TEST_POSIX_RDTIME
keys: {wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}
keys: {wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}{wait 250}
match: contains
timeout: 300
emulators: mame
---
rdtime: D, 32 KB written in calls of 4 KB: within 1000 ms: yes
rdtime: A, KERNEL.BIN read in calls of 15 KB: within {...} ms: yes
rdtime: B, 45 KB written in calls of 15 KB: within 8000 ms: yes
rdtime: B, 45 KB read back in calls of 15 KB: within 1000 ms: yes
rdtime: C, NUMBERS.TXT read in calls of 15 KB: within 500 ms: yes
rdtime: C, NUMBERS.TXT read in calls of 512 bytes: within 1100 ms: yes
rdtime: C, NUMBERS.TXT 20 times in calls of 15 KB: within 2600 us a sector: yes
rdtime: done
